 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Korean meals often finish a jeongol by cooking something in the leftover broth.</li><li>Add a portion of udon or a bowl of cooked rice, simmer for 2–3 minutes and stir in a beaten egg.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Finish with noodles or rice"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Use at least three kinds for different textures: meaty (oyster, king oyster), firm (shiitake, brown) and delicate (enoki, shimeji).</li><li>About <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"400\" data-b=\"450\" data-s=\"0\">400–450 g</span> of mushrooms in total suits 2 people.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Any mushrooms work"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>For gentle heat, stir <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"vol\" data-u=\"tsp\" data-a=\"0.5\" data-s=\"0\">½ tsp</span> gochugaru into the broth, or add a sliced mild green chilli with the spring onion.</p>",
   "title_html": "A little warmth"
  }
 ],
